  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "Atomic models",
      "html": "<ul><li><b>Dalton</b>: atom indivisible sphere.</li><li><b>Thomson</b> (plum-pudding): positive sphere with embedded electrons.</li><li><b>Rutherford</b> (nuclear): dense positive nucleus, electrons orbit.</li><li><b>Bohr</b>: electrons in fixed circular orbits with quantised energy; photon emitted when electron drops from higher to lower level, E_photon = hf = E_higher \u2212 E_lower.</li></ul><p>Atomic spectra are line spectra characteristic of each element.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Z = number of protons.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Atomic number Z is the number of<br/><b>A:</b> protons</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Atomic number Z is the number of \u2014 protons.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Who proposed the nuclear model?",
          "a": "Ernest Rutherford."
        },
        {
          "q": "Bohr postulate: energy is\u2026",
          "a": "quantised (discrete)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "X-rays",
      "html": "<p>X-rays are produced when high-energy electrons decelerate in a heavy target (tungsten). They are electromagnetic waves of very short wavelength (~10\u207b\u00b9\u2070 m).</p><p>Uses: medical imaging, crystallography, industrial inspection, treatment (radiotherapy).</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Bremsstrahlung and characteristic X-rays.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> X-rays are produced when<br/><b>A:</b> fast electrons hit a heavy target</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>X-rays are produced when \u2014 fast electrons hit a heavy target.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Give one danger of X-rays.",
          "a": "Ionising damage to cells; can cause cancer."
        },
        {
          "q": "Give one use of X-rays.",
          "a": "Diagnostic radiography."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Radioactivity",
      "html": "<p>Spontaneous emission from unstable nuclei of \u03b1, \u03b2 and \u03b3 radiation.</p><ul><li><b>Alpha (\u03b1)</b>: helium nucleus, +2e, weakly penetrating (paper stops it), strongly ionising.</li><li><b>Beta (\u03b2)</b>: fast electron, \u2212e, more penetrating (stopped by aluminium), less ionising.</li><li><b>Gamma (\u03b3)</b>: EM photon, no charge, highly penetrating (thick lead), least ionising.</li></ul><p><b>Decay law</b>: N = N\u2080 e^(\u2212\u03bbt). Half-life T\u00bd = ln 2 / \u03bb \u2248 0.693/\u03bb.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Gold-foil experiment.</li><li>Quantised orbits.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> The scientist who proposed the nuclear model of the atom is<br/><b>A:</b> Rutherford</p><p><b>Q:</b> Bohr introduced the idea that electrons occupy<br/><b>A:</b> fixed quantised orbits</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "After 3 half-lives, fraction remaining?",
          "a": "(1/2)\u00b3 = 1/8."
        },
        {
          "q": "An isotope has half-life 5 days. Fraction after 15 days?",
          "a": "1/8."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fission and fusion",
      "html": "<p><b>Nuclear fission</b>: heavy nucleus (e.g. U-235) splits into lighter nuclei, releasing neutrons and energy. Powers reactors and atomic bombs.</p><p><b>Nuclear fusion</b>: light nuclei (e.g. hydrogen) combine to form heavier nuclei, releasing enormous energy. Powers the Sun and stars.</p><p>Mass\u2013energy equivalence: E = mc\u00b2.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>H \u2192 He fusion.</li><li>Neutrons + heat.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which process powers the Sun?<br/><b>A:</b> Fusion</p><p><b>Q:</b> Nuclear fission of U-235 releases<br/><b>A:</b> neutrons and energy</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Nuclear fission of U-235 releases \u2014 neutrons and energy.</li><li>Which of these is a nuclear fusion reaction \u2014 H + H \u2192 He.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which process powers the Sun?",
          "a": "Nuclear fusion."
        },
        {
          "q": "Which reaction is used in current nuclear power stations?",
          "a": "Fission of U-235."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Semiconductors and diodes",
      "html": "<p><b>Conductors</b> (metals): overlapping bands, low resistivity.<br><b>Insulators</b>: wide energy gap.<br><b>Semiconductors</b>: small energy gap (~1 eV) \u2014 silicon, germanium.</p><p><b>Intrinsic</b>: pure semiconductor. <b>Extrinsic</b>: doped.<br>n-type: doped with pentavalent (P, As); majority carriers are electrons.<br>p-type: doped with trivalent (B, Al); majority carriers are holes.</p><p><b>p\u2013n junction diode</b>: conducts when forward-biased, blocks when reverse-biased \u2014 used to rectify AC.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Four diodes.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Full-wave bridge rectifier uses<br/><b>A:</b> 4</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Silicon doped with phosphorus is what type?",
          "a": "n-type."
        },
        {
          "q": "What is the main use of a diode?",
          "a": "Rectification (AC \u2192 DC)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Radioactivity, decay and half-life",
      "html": "<p><b>Radioactivity, decay and half-life</b> is a key part of Modern Physics (SSS 3, Physics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Decay law : N = N\u2080 e^(\u2212\u03bbt).</li><li>Half-life T\u00bd = ln 2 / \u03bb \u2248 0.693/\u03bb.</li><li>Bq = decays per second.</li><li>\u00b9\u2074C half-life 5730 y.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> After 4 half-lives, fraction of isotope remaining:<br/><b>A:</b> 1/16 \u2014 (1/2)\u2074.</p><p><b>Q:</b> An isotope has T\u00bd=10 days. Fraction left after 30 days:<br/><b>A:</b> 1/8 \u2014 3 half-lives \u2192 1/8.</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>After 4 half-lives, fraction of isotope remaining: \u2014 1/16.</li><li>Half-life T\u00bd = \u2014 ln 2/\u03bb.</li><li>Half-wave rectification uses \u2014 1 diode.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "After 4 half-lives, fraction of isotope remaining:",
          "a": "1/16 \u2014 (1/2)\u2074."
        },
        {
          "q": "An isotope has T\u00bd=10 days. Fraction left after 30 days:",
          "a": "1/8 \u2014 3 half-lives \u2192 1/8."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
